    "volunteers": "291",
    "mission": "Our mission is to inspire hope and offer opportunities so youth facing systemic obstacles can achieve positive futures.\n\nBUILD envisions a future where every young person in Chicago thrives in the care of a community filled with support and opportunity. Free from systemic obstacles, inspired by creativity and excellence, and empowered to realize their full potential, young people build lives that actively contribute to the safety, stability, and well-being of their communities.",
    "program_name": "",
    "program_description": "In Chicago and nationwide, there has been a welcome surge of funding for Community Violence Intervention (CVI) work, which identifies individuals \u201cat the highest immediate risk of perpetrating or being victims of gun violence\u201d (statistically, 18+ with arrest records and gun violence in their networks) and wraps them in integrated services like job training, life coaching, and mental health care. CVI is crucial work, serving a population in dire need and immediate danger, and BUILD is proud to be part of the citywide SC2 initiative, scaling up this work across the West Side. \n\nHowever, to sustain CVI successes and reduce violence in the long term, we must also expand upstream into prevention work - engaging younger youth before they age into gun violence and an arrest record. Risk factors for gun violence begin early, and they are cumulative; the more we can intervene earlier with positive youth development programs (PYD) and counteract these risk factors with protective factors \u2013 like positive peer networks, caring adults, social skills, emotional regulation, and school success \u2013 the more we shrink the pipeline and fewer adults need traditional CVI. It is more efficient anti-violence work, with a broader impact - a stitch in time really can save nine. Risk factors start accumulating young, but protective factors do too. \n\nBUILD is seeking support for our upstream violence prevention programs, where we embed BUILD mentors, therapists, and advocates full-time into elementary schools, to wrap vulnerable youth (and their families) in services and support.  \n\nThese Prevention Teams provide safe, structured, nurturing spaces  - in school, after school, and during school breaks. They help youth build positive skills and relationships, get mentally healthy, and find their creative voice. They reinforce academic knowledge gained during the school day, model effective study habits, foster social emotional learning, and immerse youth in art, music, sports, STEM, and recreational activities \u2013 all with individual and group mentoring from caring adults from similar backgrounds who have walked in their shoes.  \n\n BUILD staff collaborate with the school teachers and social workers to get to know young people and their caretakers over time, earning their trust as they develop a deeper understanding of each youth and their household context. Taking each youth as an individual, BUILD teams assess how to best to support them: mental health care, case management, legal navigation, violence intervention, clinical therapy, gang recruitment interruption, housing support, whatever is needed. If BUILD cannot meet a need ourselves from within BUILD, we coordinate with our partners, but maintain the core relationship. \n\n  \n\nBeginning with this core relationship, we start to build a \u201cvillage\u201d of services, opportunities, and positive people around vulnerable youth and their families. And just as gun violence creates \u201ccascading harm\u201d to society, every young life preserved is a cascade of harm that never begins, replaced with the cascading good of a youth realizing their potential  - positively impacting schools, homes, and neighborhoods. This is the \"Violence Prevention Village\" model - it is a hopeful projection, and a critical path to meaningful, generational change.  \n\n  \n\nTarget Population: ages 6-14 \n\nWhile BUILD\u2019s neighborhoods have many assets, vibrant cultures, and dedicated groups of residents, nonprofits, schools, churches, and other stakeholders working together to improve quality of life, they contend with systemic challenges ranging from poverty and gun violence to limited access to mental health services and healthy food. Youth come to BUILD in high-risk situations and with complex needs, including those who are: involved with the justice system or gang-affiliated; housing and/or food insecure; experiencing academic or behavior issues, truant, or have dropped out of school altogether; and experiencing violence-related trauma. In FY24, BUILD served 7,500 unduplicated youth - 58% were African American, 23% Latinx, 9% multi-racial, and 9% unreported; 52% female and 47% male; 100% attended schools where all qualified for free or reduced-price school meals as of 2020, the last time ISBE released school data. 99% of BUILD youth have witnessed violence in their lives, 63% in the last 12 months. 83% have friends or family already affiliated with gangs, 39% are having disciplinary issues at school, 49% report being bullied.  \n\nKey Program Components  \n\nIn-School and Out of School BUILD activities are focused on helping youth develop four key skill areas:  \n\n1) succeeding in school and building healthy personal relationships - homework help, test prep, STEM, cultural and recreational field trips, and family nights.  \n\n2) building resilience, mental and physical health - intensive individual and group mentoring, sports leagues (flag football, basketball, volleyball, softball), peace circles, and social emotional learning (SEL) \n\n3) cultivating the creative spirit with pursuits like as arts, music, drama and dance;  \n\n4) leadership experience, so critical to their future success \u2013 these activities include civic education and community service projects like neighborhood clean-ups, and putting together hygiene kits for residents.",
