    "mission": "Mission: To build an inclusive economy by developing employment pathways to advance transformative individual and community success.  \n\nVision: To fuel a courageous national movement to eradicate relational and financial poverty.",
    "program_name": "",
    "program_description": "Founded in 1991, Cara Collective\u2019s mission is to build an inclusive economy by developing employment pathways to advance transformative individual and community success. Through our four entities \u2013 Cara, Cleanslate, Cara Connects, and Cara Plus \u2013 we engage job seekers, employers, and other organizations in Chicago and across the country to break the cycle of poverty. Over the past 33 years, we have moved the needle on poverty alleviation, gaining employment for more than 9,500 people and creating more than 15,000 jobs.\n\nTarget Population\nCara serves motivated job seekers who live below the federal poverty level (97%), have experienced episodic homelessness, and/or are unemployed or underemployed. Many of the barriers our participants face, range from criminal background, substance abuse, and low levels of education, and stem from systemic conditions that create and perpetuate inconsistent access to opportunity. Cara participants come to us primarily from communities on the South and West Sides of Chicago, with the top five community areas being Auburn Gresham, Chatham, Englewood, South Shore, and Roseland. The unemployment rate for Black residents in Illinois remains high for individuals over 20 years of age. In April 2023, Black unemployment rate in Illinois was at 10.2%, more than three times that of the White unemployment rate of 3.2% and more than double the rate of Hispanic unemployment at 4.6%. Additionally, Black households in Chicago on average have ten times less wealth than white households. Of the people we serve, 82% of our job seekers identify as Black/African American, 7% as Hispanic, 7% as white, and 4% as other.  30% of our participants do not have a high school diploma/GED, and 79% have a conviction history. \n\nKey Activities \nCara recruits participants from sources including self-referrals, word of mouth from alumni, and referrals from more than 80 community organizations. Once onboarded, participants gain traction in their journey through a variety of training, coaching, and transitional job experiences designed to build skills while also building self-awareness around their full potential. Our programs are grounded in the ever-evolving needs of our job seekers while remaining aligned with the needs of the workforce. Participants enter one of two program pathways: 1) Cara Core Program (CCP) or 2) Cara Accelerator Program (CAP). In each, job seekers complete a 4-week program. CCP is delivered over 4 days per week and totals 26 workshops while CAP is delivered over 2 days per week with a total of 10 workshops.\n\n\u2022 Workforce Development Training: Our cohort style model focuses on social-emotional learning and workplace competencies. Job seekers attend workshops on personal and professional development, digital literacy, and workplace competencies including professionalism, time management, teamwork, conflict management, and communication. \n\u2022 Supportive Services: Cara provides supportive services including housing, financial and digital literacy, mental health, childcare, and more. Where we do not offer support in-house, we refer to partners. Separately, to support job seekers path to situational stability, CCP participants receive stipends of up to $500 total throughout the program and CAP participants receive paid training for their time in the classroom.\n\u2022 Social Enterprises: Our two employment social enterprises (ESE\u2019s) Cleanslate (an exterior maintenance business) and Cara Connects (a mission driven staffing firm) create jobs and provide temporary employment opportunities for participants experiencing the greatest barriers to meaningful employment. Both ESE\u2019s allow participants to gain on-the-job experience, while earning income and building their resume. Our headquarters for Cleanslate is in Englewood, a community area where many of our job seekers are also from. This location was determined intentionally, to make our services more accessible to job seekers on Chicago\u2019s South Side.\n\u2022 Job Placement: With over 70 employment partners, we have a diversity of employment opportunities to match the diversity of candidates who enter our programs. We also employ strategies that keep us relevant to shifts in the market. Key Employment Partners include organizations such as BMO, CTA, Advocate Aurora Health, and Freedman Seating Company.\n\u2022 Retention Support: Participants are assigned a Coach within the first week of the program who provides support throughout their Cara journey and through one year in a long-term job to support with job retention.\n\nCara\u2019s Expansion Arm: Cara Plus, leverages our experience in workforce development to focus on practice change at both the practitioner level to improve workforce development interventions and at the employer level to create more opportunities for untapped talent.",
