    "volunteers": "342",
    "mission": "Mission: Center on Halsted advances community and secures the health and well-being of the LGBTQ+ people of Chicagoland and throughout the Midwest.  \n\nVision: A thriving lesbian, gay, bisexual, transgender and queer community, living powerfully in supportive inclusive environments.",
    "program_name": "",
    "program_description": "Primary Focus \n\nWith the leadership of its new CEO, Joli Robinson, Center on Halsted (COH) is reinforcing its role as a cornerstone of the LGBTQ+ community in Chicagoland, expanding services, fostering new partnerships, and deepening its commitment to equity and inclusion. COH respectfully requests $50,000 in general operating support. Your generous support will contribute to the long-term sustainability of the organization as the organization refocuses on community need and advances strategic initiatives, such as planning an expansion of critical, affirming services for unhoused LGBTQ+ youth, particularly those who are Black, Brown, transgender or nonbinary. We are working to address systemic inequities faced by LGBTQ+ individuals through a holistic approach to human services that fosters health and wellness, financial stability, basic needs, safety, and mental health through an equity lens.   \n\nTarget Population  \n\nCenter on Halsted serves all LGBTQ+ people and allies in the Chicago area, with a focus on those who are most vulnerable due to age, race, education, HIV status, etc. Disparities for LGBTQ+ communities are present in all the target areas of our programming. LGBTQ+ individuals face higher rates of mental health disorders, substance use disorders, suicide, and sexually transmitted infections (U.S. Department of Health and Human Services, n.d.). The population lacks access to affirming care and faces discrimination and stigma exacerbated by current anti-LGBTQ+ sentiment and politics (Human Rights Campaign, 2023). In the LGBTQ+ South Side Needs Assessment Final Report (June 2021), respondents rated \u201cAssistance with Mental Health Treatment/Counseling\u201d as the number one service need (59.5%), followed by \u201cLGBTQ-specific services\u201d (47.1%), which includes gender-affirming care and culturally competent health care, including mental health services (p.35).  \n\nAt the heart of COH's work is our Youth Programs, one of the earliest initiatives to take shape in the modern-day LGBTQ+ movement. Young people are one of the most vulnerable populations. Programs to support LGBTQ+ youth as they seek safety, community, affirmation, and space to understand their own identities have been at the heart of Center on Halsted\u2019s programming since it was started as Gay Horizons in 1973. According to the Trevor Project, 75% of LGBTQ+ young people reported experiencing discrimination due to their sexual or gender identities. LGBTQ+ young people report feeling unsafe at school (59%). They have higher rates of homelessness (120% higher than the non-LGBTQ+ population), suicide attempts (3.5 times as high as non-LGBTQ+ youth). Transgender youth are particularly vulnerable to societal and family rejection and are at greater danger due to public attacks (GLAAD Media Reference Guide, 11th Edition). \n\nAlmost all of Center on Halsted\u2019s youth clients have experienced trauma and are continuously learning ways to manage trauma and facilitate self-care and healthy behaviors. There is often a family history of abuse; homelessness; long-term unemployment linked to anti-LGBTQ+ bias in hiring practices; and/or experiences of interpersonal, sexual, or intimate partner violence. In addition to direct service to youth aged 12-24, COH serves younger children with their families. Many youth clients come to COH with the support of their caregivers; however, the adults accompanying youth frequently feel ill-equipped to address the broad range of behavioral, emotional, and mental health needs their loved ones are experiencing. \n\nKey Activities: \n\nBy design, as a \"one-stop\" center, COH meets the multiple needs of its clients. COH provides free to low-cost services to meet the needs of clients across all stages of their lives in LGBTQ+-affirming, safe, and welcoming environments with programs for the entire community. COH programs and services are open to all people regardless of economic standing, gender identity, race, or sexual orientation. Programs support clients\u2019 behavioral, psychological, food, housing, health-related, and social needs: \n\nBehavioral Health Services, including individualized and group therapy and services for survivors of violence; \n\nCommunity and Cultural Programming for people of all ages, including performing arts partnerships; volleyball, pickle ball, and other sports; and social programming; \n\nCommunity Technology Center (Cyber Center); \n\nHIV Resources and Prevention, including testing and Pre-Exposure Prophylaxis (PrEP) outreach; HIV Resource Hub, in collaboration with the AIDS Foundation Chicago; and the State of Illinois HIV Hotline; \n\nCenter on Cottage Grove satellite program site in Woodlawn; \n\nPride in Aging and affordable housing for seniors (Center on Addison and Town Hall Apartments); \n\nSilver Fork (workforce development in culinary arts); \n\nYouth Programs, including social-emotional programming; and \n\nProfessional development including the Inclusive Futures Leadership Institute and Midwest Pride, a convening of LGBTQ+ professionals. \n\nAs social, behavioral health, and economic disparities intensify\u2014driven by the growing backlash against trans individuals and the broader LGBTQ+ community\u2014we anticipate these challenges will only deepen. In this climate, it is more critical than ever that our programs are created by and for those most vulnerable. In response, our strategic priorities for FY26 include: \n\nIncreasing the reach and quality of operations at COH; its satellite location, Center on Cottage Grove (COCG); and citywide\u2014addressing unmet demand for social services, including a reevaluation of the Youth Housing program to ensure that the program fills a gap in the safety net, is effective, and maximizes resources. \n\nDeepening the community activation of COH's spaces by reconnecting with organizations that have utilized the Center's spaces and establishing new relationships to benefit clients. In 2025, our nonprofit partners Pride Arts and Lambda Legal\u2019s Midwest regional office both moved their offices to Center on Halsted. At COCG, Life is Work is holding weekly trans drop-ins, and Nourishing Hope is providing a monthly online market. \n\nCreating increased awareness through visible leadership engagement is part of a marketing and communications strategy utilizing various media, culturally specific messaging, and community-building activities and events for clients, staff, and other external constituents. We are also marketing COH and its programs to LGBTQ+ people moving to Illinois, migrants, and others new to Chicago. \n\nProviding training, public education and professional development to LGBTQ+ and allied professionals and acting as a convenor of LGBTQ+-serving organizations to provide support and training through events like the Inclusive Futures Leadership Institute, a professional development program for 30 people, and Midwest Pride, a 2.5-day conference to support staff, board members, and volunteers from LGBTQ+-serving organizations regionally.",
