    "part_time_staff": "4",
    "volunteers": "100",
    "mission": "Chicago Coalition to End Homelessness (CCH) builds community power and advances racial equity through organizing, advocacy, legal assistance, and education. CCH strives to prevent and end homelessness across Chicago and Illinois because housing is a human right. We take the word \u201ccoalition\u201d seriously, partnering with grassroots leaders with lived experience of homelessness, service providers, advocacy groups, and other community partners to create lasting change. Together, we work towards a world where everyone has a safe, welcoming, and permanent place to call home.",
    "program_name": "",
    "program_description": "CCH has organized and advocated to prevent and end homelessness since 1980, with our tactics evolving based on changing community needs. Today, CCH\u2019s key issue areas include housing justice, youth homelessness, educational rights, reentry, public benefits, civil rights, and racial equity. We lead community outreach and strategic advocacy campaigns that address the immediate needs of people impacted by poverty while developing long-term solutions to homelessness.\n\nDirect Service Work\n\nCommunity Education & Outreach: Through outreach, CCH educates people experiencing homelessness on their rights and options and engages those interested in advocacy. We write and distribute 20+ types of know-your-rights materials and offer free trainings to service providers and attorneys. Our Speakers Bureau, comprised of grassroots leaders who have experienced homelessness, educates about 2,000 people annually.\n\nThe Law Project: Ours is the only legal aid program in Illinois exclusively focused on the needs of people experiencing or at risk of homelessness. Legal staff also run Youth Futures, a mobile legal clinic for youth ages 14-24.\n\nEdrika Fulford Mutual Aid Fund: Designed and managed by CCH grassroots leaders, the fund has distributed more than $500,000 in emergency grants to 1,100 households since 2020.\n\nHorizons creative writing program: Offered on-site at shelters, participants develop story-telling skills while fostering community connection.\n\nScholarship Program: Provides renewable awards of $4,000 to high school seniors impacted by homelessness. \n\nStreetlight Chicago: Fully redeveloped in 2024, this free app and website connects unaccompanied youth to up-to-date resources. \n\nPolicy & Advocacy Work\n\nCCH partners with advocacy organizations, service providers, and people with lived experience to drive local and statewide policy initiatives that address homelessness and increase funding for human services. \n\nCCH works to implement an annual legislative agenda that advances statewide policy solutions for housing, education, and public benefits. We also advocate for equitable budgeting and improvements to statewide housing programs. \n\nOur Bring Chicago Home campaign advocates for a dedicated revenue stream to create housing and services for people experiencing homelessness. \n\nThe Reentry Project addresses housing and job barriers for justice-involved people. \n\nThe Homeless Youth Committee mobilizes 30+ organizational partners, young people, and advocates to address the unique challenges of unaccompanied youth. \n\nA CPS Focus Group works to increase resources and improve services for students who attend Chicago Public Schools.\n\nCCH manages a Homelessness Data Project which advocates for HUD to amend its definition of homelessness to include people living doubled-up to determine eligibility for services.",
