    "part_time_staff": "0",
    "volunteers": "12",
    "mission": "City Colleges of Chicago Foundation (CCCF) is a 501c3 organization that partners with the philanthropic community in supporting the whole student through scholarships, wrap-around services, and innovative programming that empowers socioeconomic upward mobility of City Colleges of Chicago (CCC) students from all backgrounds. CCC is recognized as the city\u2019s most accessible higher education engine of socioeconomic mobility and racial equity, empowering all Chicagoans to take part in building a stronger, more just city.  It plays a crucial role in providing affordable education and workforce development opportunities to residents of Chicago and surrounding communities. CCCF is governed by its own board of directors, and chartered to provide financially for the educational needs of the City Colleges of Chicago that cannot be met through state aid or student tuition income.",
    "program_name": "",
    "program_description": "Sixty-nine percent (69%) of CCC student respondents report facing at least one form of insecurity, significantly higher than other two-year colleges' survey results. Fifty percent (50%) reported food insecurity. Fifty nine percent (59%) reported housing insecurity. Sixteen percent (16%) reported experiencing homelessness. Thirty two percent (32%) of students do not use a personal computer to do their coursework. Eighteen percent (18%) of students say they missed assignments due to internet/technology. Twelve percent (12%) do not utilize home internet access.\n\nCity College of Chicago students experience challenges that their more affluent peers may never experience and puts them at risk of dropping classes or stopping out altogether, preventing persistence and success. Addressing our students' basic needs insecurity is an imperative. If we help students thrive personally, they can thrive academically, preparing for upwardly mobile careers and family-sustaining wages, and becoming basic-needs-secure for life! \n\nNationwide, studies have shown that addressing students' BNI leads to stronger academic outcomes. Historically CCC and its partners have invested in supporting students' basic needs including wide-ranging strategies such as mental health supports via campus Wellness Centers and evening/weekend telehealth, food pantries at all seven colleges, limited housing options, discounted CTA Ventra cards, free laptops and Wi-Fi, and limited access to emergency financial assistance. Still, significant gaps exist in CCC's ability to meet students' basic needs, and philanthropic support is needed to help students achieve their goals and to help CCC to achieve its vision as an engine of racial equity and socioeconomic mobility across communities. \n\nCity Colleges requests a total of $505,000 over 3-years as an investment in CCC\u2019s Thrive Strategy: Infrastructure Program, a component of a comprehensive strategy to strengthen and augment the continuum of Basic Needs Supports to address Food Insecurity, Housing Insecurity, Emergency Financial Assistance and Public Benefits Connection as well and preparing the foundation for a more scalable, sustainable BNI support infrastructure at CCC.  A crucial first step toward addressing BNI at CCC is to build the human capital and technological supports required for CCC to have the capacity to move forward its suite of interventions and initiatives to all seven colleges including its five satellites.\n\nHuman Capital - CCC will hire a District Director of Basic Needs, a role that will report to the Executive Director of Student Wellness and will be charged to expand CCC\u2019s BNI response framework, implement programming across the district while collecting data to inform interventions, learning, building scalable operations, and continuous improvement. \n\nTechnology: Basic Needs Distribution Software - CCC will purchase-update technology and software systems, to facilitate effectively process internal emergency fund applications data collection and including initial start-up implementation costs and annual technology vendor fees. \n\nOf the total funds, $125,000 will provide support during the remainder of the current fiscal year (FY25) and $190,000 for FY26 and FY2. In terms of sustainability, we anticipate that as student retention increases over next 3 years, tuition revenue will fund additional infrastructure costs into perpetuity.",
