    "mission": "Erie Neighborhood House\u2019s mission is to empower our community\u2014Latinx immigrants alongside individuals and families of all backgrounds\u2014through education, access to critical services and advocacy, working together to create a just and inclusive society. Our vision is a home with no borders, impacting lives to create powerful communities.",
    "program_name": "",
    "program_description": "The primary focus of our organization is to support immigrant and low-income families in Chicago and empower them to build powerful communities. When we were first opened our doors in 1870\u2014when Chicago was only 33 years old\u2014we served the Dutch immigrants making their homes in the city. Chicago\u2019s populations have shifted with the times, and we have evolved alongside it to stay true to our mission. Today, we primarily serve Latinx families and individuals from Chicago\u2019s West and Southwest Sides, offering a spectrum of programs and services that address the complex needs of our community. We categorize our programs into five service areas: Children & Youth, Adult Education & Training, Community Resources & Empowerment, Legal Services, and Mental Health & Community Wellness.  Funding from Knight Impact Partners will support Erie Neighborhood House\u2019s wraparound programming model, particularly as it relates to families, health, human services, and workforce development.\n\nBy ensuring that children and youth in our programs are cared for and receiving developmentally appropriate instruction and guidance, Erie House provides parents a valuable and much-needed resource in the community for their families. Early Childhood Education (ECE) for children ages 0 to 5, and Expanded Learning Programs (ELP) for children and youth ages 6 to 18. ECE provides on-site holistic education and development programming for pre-school aged children (ages 0-5); home visiting for expectant mothers and children ages birth to 3 years old; and mental health services for children up to age 5 and their families. Expanded Learning Programs (ELP) offers  three year-round, age-appropriate programming options for children and youth: (1) School Age Program, which offers an array of enrichment services for youth ages 5-12 to include elementary literacy, academic support mentoring, social-emotional health support, health education, physical activity, and field trips; (2) Youth Options Unlimited, which improves academic attainment and life skills and offers safe, recreational alternatives for at-risk youth ages 12-18; and (3) READS, which is a tutoring program designed to improve 1st-3rd graders\u2019 literacy skills for children referred to Erie House by our partner schools in Little Village neighborhood of South Lawndale.\n\nOur Children & Youth services also provide essential childcare for parents who are attempting to further their education or pursue employment opportunities that can lead to family-sustaining wages. Our Adult Education and Training services provide foundational academic and professional programs that prepare adults for long-term success. Programming spans English as a Second Language instruction, Adult Basic Education, community literacy and civics classes, one-on-one and small group tutoring, and basic, intermediate, and advanced technology education.  Participation in these programs leads to stronger reading and comprehension skills, increased facility with English as a Second Language, completion of industry-specific training and certification courses, and in some instances American citizenship.\n\nThe areas of Community Resources & Empowerment and Mental Health & Community Wellness provide clients with case management and accompaniment to improve people\u2019s success at accessing public benefits, basic healthcare, and housing. We host workshops on health and wellness, nutrition, financial literacy, tenant rights, and transitioning from tenancy to home ownership. Our bilingual behavioral health therapy services help children, teens, and adults to meet their overall wellness goals. Programming encourages families to be mindful, eat well, and stay active. Community health workers also promote healthcare utilization by providing case management support and accompanying clients throughout their healthcare journey.",
