    "volunteers": "200",
    "mission": "Hope Chicago\u2019s mission is to create pathways to success for multiple generations of Chicagoans through equitable access to higher education. We provide scholarships and wraparound services to students and one parent or guardian as part of our two-generation approach. Hope\u2019s bold vision is education and economic equity for all Chicago students and families, achieved through a holistic support model throughout high school, key transitions, and postsecondary pathways.",
    "program_name": "",
    "program_description": "Chicago\u2019s neighborhoods are divided by economic status, which all too often aligns with race. The South and West side communities Hope Chicago serves face severe systemic barriers to wealth creation with 32% of Black families and 24% of Latino families in Chicago living in poverty. Further, ample evidence shows that higher education is a key driver of upward economic mobility, yet, many Black and Latino families in our city lack access to the necessary resources to persist through postsecondary pathways. These disparities in access are holding families back and contributing to the vast and continuously widening wealth gap. Hope Chicago\u2019s unique two-generation model, the first of its kind in Chicago, is a comprehensive approach to removing barriers and increasing access to postsecondary pathways. Our theory of action is that increased postsecondary access leads to higher rates of enrollment and degree or credential attainment, resulting in better job opportunities, more earning potential, and increased household wealth. By providing supports and resources to students and their parents simultaneously, we will have a more significant impact, faster and at scale. Achieving debt-free higher education and/or training credentials has the power to fuel economic and social mobility for individuals, families, and, importantly, their communities. \n\nThrough last-dollar scholarships, Hope Chicago completely removes the debt burden for our Scholars, enabling them to attend a two or four-year university, certificate program, or vocational training debt-free. This project centers on our structure of wraparound support to ensure that students are prepared and supported at both the high school and college levels. At each of our partner high schools, Hope Chicago Student Success Coaches work day-to-day as an extension of the postsecondary leadership team, providing career advising, and academic and transition support. At the college level, each higher education partner provides a Campus Contact (CC), an on-campus resource for Scholars. In partnership with Hope Chicago Program Managers, Campus Contacts provide academic advising and progress monitoring while creating a community for Scholars on campus to ensure they are on the road to completing their programs or degrees. \n\nThrough our CORE program, focusing on career readiness, Hope Scholars are matched with top Chicago companies that provide paid summer internship opportunities. CORE is designed to expose Hope Scholars to various industries and real-world careers that challenge them, all the while opening up avenues for professions and future employment. We work with students to ensure that they either secure an internship, job, or summer classes to stay on track.\n\nOne parent or guardian of each Hope Scholar is also eligible to pursue debt-free postsecondary education. Through our Parent Scholar Program, parents or guardians choose a two or four-year university, vocational degree or certificate, or a workforce or job training program. Career and technical guidance is provided to ensure that parents have the unique support they need to be placed in jobs that will help create upward mobility for their families. All parents, regardless of whether or not they attend college or a vocational program, engage with Hope Chicago on a variety of fronts and receive wraparound supports, because we know students are far more likely to be academically successful when families are stable and doing well.",
