    "mission": "I.c.stars is revolutionizing access into the technology ecosystem through a transformative, learning experience that connects talented adults to tech industry and civic leaders. These connections lead to individual, business, and community transformation as program graduates launch and build their tech-related careers.",
    "program_name": "",
    "program_description": "Twenty-five years ago, Sandee Kastrul, a high school science teacher, co-founded i.c.stars after discovering that her brightest students were taking low-wage jobs due to a lack of support and opportunities. With the \u201cdot-com\u201d industry booming, she connected with local leaders to create a two-year workforce technology program that builds a pathway for career-driven and passionate adults into the tech industry. Today, i.c.stars works in Chicago, Kansas City, and Milwaukee, with alumni holding leadership positions at Fortune 100 companies, serving on non-profit boards, and dozens becoming serial entrepreneurs as web designers, database managers, and cybersecurity consultants.\n\nI.c.stars\u2019 work drives toward three organizational goals. They are:\n- deliver a project-based model that will prepare participants to be workplace and community leaders. I.c.stars will track program enrollment and skill acquisition.\n- reduce unemployment rates in disconnected communities by supporting program graduates in their job search. I.c.stars will track the speed to placement and earnings increases.\n- cultivate civically engaged leaders. I.c.stars will track volunteer experiences, pursuit of higher education and certifications, and career advancement.\n\ni.c.stars recruits career-driven adults who are unemployed or underemployed and who have not had opportunities for formal education beyond high school. Our program is open to all individuals, and we are proud to have a participant base that reflects the diverse experiences of our city locations. Over 95% of participants identify as people of color, over 40% are women, and more than 40% care for children. Nearly 40% are under the age of 26, and 46% live in zipcodes with 10% or more of the population living in poverty.\n\nOur two-year program is divided into three periods: a 14-week training period, a 90-day bridge period, and a 20-month residency period. During the 14-week training period, a corporate sponsor provides a real-world challenge that the cohort (20-25 participants) works on in small groups. Workshops develop business, leadership, and technology skills that provide participants everything they need to present an initial concept during week 4 and a final concept during week 10. Also during this period, participants receive weekly stipends ($300-$350, depending on the city location) and case management referrals to help reduce barriers. Participants who complete all learning objectives progress to the 90-day bridge period, during which staff and volunteers support their job search. Finally, throughout the residency period, participants attend networking events and professional development opportunities, such as financial planning, tech certification courses, and continued barrier-reduction services.",
