    "mission": "Mission: Elevate the well-being of the Mexican community\n\nVision: Expanding what\u2019s possible",
    "program_name": "",
    "program_description": "Latinos Progresando\u2019s (LP) mission is to elevate the well-being of the Mexican community. LP first opened its doors in 1998 to provide accessible, professional immigration legal services to Chicago\u2019s immigrant community. Since then, LP has broadened its reach while also deepening its roots, offering a full spectrum of services: corridor and community development, small business support services, resident leadership programs, coalition building, and Mexican cultural programming. Across the range of LP\u2019s work, the organization intentionally engages diverse audiences, namely Marshall Square residents, the broader immigrant community, local businesses, local schools, nonprofit organizations, and emerging/established Mexican leaders. \n\nLP\u2019s place-based work, including our community development work, focuses on the eastern subset of Chicago\u2019s South Lawndale community, historically known as Marshall Square. Home to a thriving Mexican immigrant community, Marshall Square has a rich cultural history that is anchored by several community assets: community-minded schools, industrious small businesses, a collaborative nonprofit community, and a local health district. Still, as documented in the Marshall Square Resource Network Impact Report: A Blueprint for Neighborhood Investment, Marshall Square has faced a history of disinvestment that outpaces other Chicago communities, along with systemic exclusions due to language barriers, immigration status, and education attainment gaps. While one in five Chicagoans identifies as Mexican, there is a remarkable lack of understanding of and tailored resources to address the Mexican community\u2019s unique needs. This reality is evident in Mexican business ownership, where the overall percentage is far below the Mexican-origin share of the population (see Fuerza Mexicana, LP\u2019s co-commissioned report on the state of Mexicans in Chicagoland).\n\nLP\u2019s work is designed to maximize community assets, uproot these and other inequities, and embody community voice throughout. LP believes that equitable community development will be achieved through community-driven approaches that leverage the collective strengths of Marshall Square residents. Below is a sampling of LP\u2019s local efforts.\n\nCommunity Development & Small Business Support - LP focuses on corridor development, placemaking, and small business support on Chicago's Cermak Commercial Corridor, a one-mile stretch from Kedzie to Western known locally as \u2018La Cermak\u2019. There are 130 locally-owned businesses directly on Cermak, and 359 businesses within a half mile radius. Building on a multi-year walkability study, a resident-led air quality monitoring program, and extensive level setting and relationship building with business owners, LP has facilitated $1.3 million of direct investment to the corridor, green infrastructure improvements, a Shop Local campaign, and ongoing direct support services to local business owners. \n\n\nThe Marshall Square Resource Network - LP is the convening agency of the Marshall Square Resource Network (MSRN), a place-based collaborative of more than 60 community organizations, schools, small businesses, and community residents. MSRN advances community-identified solutions in the areas of health, education, peace, and community response, as well as broader community development initiatives and Network infrastructure.\n\n\nResident Leadership through Social Emotional Learning - In 2015, Latinos Progresando was selected by CASEL to pilot its newly developed Social Emotional Learning (SEL) curriculum in Marshall Square schools. To date, 700 neighborhood parents have been trained in SEL basics by their peers. LP convened a cohort of parent leaders to serve as instructors and ambassadors. LP convenes the resident leadership cohort on an ongoing basis and consults with resident leaders on neighborhood issues, programming decisions, and community planning efforts. \n\n\nWhile the majority of LP\u2019s work is place-based with a hyper-local focus on Marshall Square, several efforts extend well beyond the neighborhood. Below are some recent examples.\n\nExcellerator Fund - The Excellerator Fund is a joint venture developed by Greater Auburn Gresham Development Corporation and Latinos Progresando to reimagine investment in Chicago\u2019s neighborhoods. The Excellerator Fund\u2019s community-driven grantmaking model centers Black and Mexican voices in directing unrestricted funding to Black and Mexican-led organizations, in Black and Mexican communities. Beyond grantmaking, the fund brings leaders together to forge bonds, share resources and work together for a better Chicago.  Now in its fourth year, the fund has mobilized more than $4.4 million dollars to 82 Black and Mexican-led organizations, the majority of which have budgets less than $500,000.\n\nOne Lawndale - Co-Founded by Latinos Progresando and North Lawndale Community Coordinating Council, One Lawndale is a cross-community, cross-cultural collaborative that seeks to build bridges between the historically segregated communities of North and South Lawndale. The collaborative has birthed the One Lawndale Summer Event Series and has led to the conceptualization of the Lawndale Community Campus, a collection of several capital projects within walking distance of each other which include Sinai Chicago\u2019s One Lawndale Community Care Center and the forthcoming One Lawndale Children\u2019s Discovery Center.",
