    "postal_code": "60621",
    "website": "www.margaretsvillage.org",
    "email": "",
    "phone": "773-651-8372 x 240",
    "year_founded": "1974",
    "tax_id": "36-3104655",
    "contact_first_name": "Angela",
    "contact_last_name": "Hicks",
    "contact_title": "Executive Director",
    "contact_email": "rani.averick@gmail.com",
    "leader_first_name": "Rani",
    "leader_last_name": "Averick",
    "leader_title": "Development / Grant Writer",
    "leader_email": "rani.averick@gmail.com",
    "full_time_staff": "25",
    "part_time_staff": "6",
    "volunteers": "150",
    "mission": "Margaret's Village transforms lives. We provide a transitional housing and empowerment program to end homelessness for women, children, and families on Chicago\u2019s South Side, to turn isolation and homelessness into community and self-sufficiency.  We serve approximately 400 individuals annually across two 50-bed facilities: Maria Shelter in Englewood for women and children, and Believe Shelter in South Chicago for families that may include adult men. Our residents are 90% Black, 7% Hispanic, 60% children\u2014including infants, toddlers, and teenagers. 33% arrive with some degree of mental health issues. During a typical stay of four-to-eight months, our objective is to help our residents transcend trauma, stabilize their lives, and attain safe and affordable permanent housing.\n\nWe also operate the Vincennes Senior Center, providing companionship, activities, and fresh meals for approximately 100 local seniors facing isolation and food insecurity. We are the site of a Community Garden from which we and our neighbors harvest fresh produce in season. And we host an annual Community Health Fair in the summer, and an annual Community Peace Fair in the fall.\n\nOur vision is to provide safety and security to those who need us, to be an anchor in our communities, and to collaborate with our partners to bring systemic change to Chicago\u2019s South Side.",
    "program_name": "",
    "program_description": "Each year, Margaret\u2019s Village serves approximately 400 homeless individuals for a typical stay of four to  eight months. Most are referrals from Chicago\u2019s Department of Family and Support Services. The city calls every day to transfer people from emergency shelter if beds are available. Agencies, churches, and hospitals also refer clients. And, on a typical day, 30+ individuals call seeking temporary housing.\n90% of our residents are Black, 7% are Hispanic, 60% are children. Typically, 33% arrive with some degree of mental health issues. They arrive at the lowest point in their lives, often with only the clothes on their backs or a plastic bag of belongings. We welcome all with dignity and respect. We say, \u201cYour current situation is not your conclusion. Change begins here. We expect great things.\u201d\n\nAt Margaret\u2019s Village, residents experience safe harbor, empathy, and community. With their basic needs met, they can \"exhale\" and begin the process of making positive change in their lives.  A case manager takes a personal history of each new resident to examine and address root causes and traumas that led them to us. The resident and case manager meet regularly, creating a trusted relationship where it is safe to air difficult personal issues\u2014sometimes for the first time. Together they create a personalized, strengths-based plan forward. The plan addresses obstacles and sets goals for employment, housing, education, job-training, and life-skills and enrichment programs, along with referrals to wellness, social, financial management, and legal services as needed. \n\nWith the plan in place, the resident takes the lead, while the case manager follows up regularly with the resident and partner agencies to assess progress. If progress is stalled, the case manager helps the resident uncover impediments, and the plan is adjusted as needed to provide additional helpful resources. With each small victory, the resident gains confidence and moves toward holistic stability and progress.\n\nAll residents have access to health screenings and referrals at our onsite clinics run by the UChicago Pritzker School of Medicine and Heartland Alliance Health, as well as mental/behavioral health services and referrals provided onsite by the UChicago medical school.\n\nFollowing is a sampling of our many partner organizations to whom we refer residents for services: Iman Health Clinic, SGA Youth and Family Services, Legal Aid Foundation, Heartland Alliance Health, local City Colleges, Cara, Goodwill, Jane Addams Resource Corp, Chicago Coalition for the Homeless, Teamwork Englewood, and more. Other partners and volunteers provide enrichment such as tutoring for school-age residents, art and music therapy, yoga, and parenting classes. We serve three fresh-cooked meals daily. Our warm environment offers an onsite computer lab, health and wellness seminars, musical events, holiday parties, and more.\n\nAll staff, from cooks to case managers, are trained in trauma-informed-care to foster a regenerative environment and trusted relationships. Our leadership \u2013 100% African American \u2013 and our staff are of the community we serve. Some have experienced homelessness themselves, giving them a keen understanding of our residents\u2019 lived experience.\n\nWe maintain an updated job board with instructions on how to apply. We assist with the application process and provide interview coaching. If a resident is unable to work for any reason, we help them access benefits such as Medicare, social security, and food assistance, which helps them qualify for housing that requires some form of income (not necessarily from employment).\n\nWe refer residents to independent housing agencies. We help negotiate rents. We might suggest to residents to become roommates if we see they have a good relationship. Some wish to stay local; others want a fresh--and more affordable--start elsewhere. We can help find housing in and around the Chicago area, downstate, and out of state. We research the new locale and apprise our resident(s) of local resources. When ready to move on, we provide basic household items and connect them with an organization that provides donated furniture in excellent condition.\n\nAfter a resident has left us, we formally track them for six months. They know we are always available to help them remain independent in any way we can. Former residents often visit or call for assistance, to talk, or to provide support to current residents. Some even return years later just to say thank you.\n\nThank you to Knight Impact Partners for your consideration of Margaret\u2019s Village. We would be delighted to welcome you for a site visit, to show you the home base from which our residents launch brighter tomorrows.",
