    "mission": "Mission Statement:\u202f \n\nNew Life Centers exists to connect young people and their families with Christ and Community through life-on-life relationships.\u202f \n\n\u202f\u202f \n\nVision:\u202f\u202f \n\nThe Beloved Community is a global vision envisioned by Dr. Martin Luther King Jr. as a society where all people share in the wealth of the earth, creating authentic community. It is achieved through nonviolent resistance to injustices and proactive commitment to right relationships, centering the leadership and voices of young people.",
    "program_name": "",
    "program_description": "Pan De Vida is a food pantry designed to provide for the physical needs of the community, affirm the dignity of those it serves, and bring the community together. Verenice Martinez, Director of Food Distribution Services, describes Pan De Vida: \u201cPan De Vida provides hope and healing by connecting families to fresh food, community, and relationships. Every week, families can come to our location in Little Village or one of our pop-up sites around the city to receive a variety of fresh produce, dry goods, meat, milk and dairy. Food is the vehicle, Beloved Community is the destination.\u201d \n\nPan De Vida operates four days a week in the Little Village neighborhood, twice a week in Midway, and has monthly pop-ups in Humboldt Park. Currently 1 in 5 households in Chicago experience food insecurity, and this actually increases to 1 in 4 for families with children.1 Additionally, there is a racial divide with Black and Latinx households experiencing food insecurity at higher rates than White households.2 All the communities we serve are predominately Hispanic and/or Black.3 In certain parts of Midway, up to 27.7%  of the population is living below the poverty line, and that number only increases in the other neighborhoods served with data indicating that certain census tracts within these neighborhoods are deeply impoverished: Humboldt Park ( up to 38.2%), and in Little Village that number goes as high as 39.5%.4 Many impoverished households in these neighborhoods are not receiving food stamps\u2014Humboldt Park (28.7%), South Lawndale (54.7%), and Archer Heights (73.15%).5 By targeting these neighborhoods, Pan De Vida is able to provide much needed resources. \n\nPan De Vida provides fresh produce, dairy, meat, and dry goods to serve 2,000 people weekly, ensuring access to nutritious food. Our daily operations include sourcing, stocking, and distributing food items while ensuring quality and safety standards. Through Pan De Vida, families not only receive essential food supplies but are also connected to a network of additional services. This includes assistance with wraparound supports and education programs such as English classes and training for entrepreneurs.",
