    "mission": "Mission: One Million Degrees accelerates community college students\u2019 progress on career pathways to economic mobility.\u202f   \n\nVision: OMD\u2019s 2028 vision is to be a national model for scaling holistic student supports through sustainable partnerships in service of equitable education and career outcomes for community college students.",
    "program_name": "",
    "program_description": "OMD seeks to produce cascading impacts by delivering holistic wrap around services to community college students that will improve their academic and economic outcomes. Community colleges are uniquely positioned to accelerate economic mobility for BIPOC students and students from low-income backgrounds due to their status as open access institutions, relatively low cost of attendance, and shorter time to degree completion. The long-term goal of these interventions is to drive systems wide change that will advance equity across institutions of higher education by embedding and institutionalizing programming, ultimately improving tuition revenues. Highly skilled scholars will graduate ready to step into in-demand careers capable of providing family-sustaining wages, meeting employer demands in key industries and providing economic mobility to communities that have suffered from historic academic and economic gatekeeping.    \n\nOMD provides scholars with holistic supportive services proven to address their barriers to successfully persisting through school, completing their degree, and transitioning into upwardly mobile careers. This suite of services includes:   \n\nPersonal Supports - biweekly meetings with a staff Program Coordinator (embedded on campus), focusing on both on- and off-campus resource connections, and academic and career goal setting/tracking.  \n\nProfessional and Career Supports - monthly meetings with a volunteer professional mentor; monthly workshops on work and life skills to support students personal, financial, and professional wellbeing; resume review, interview preparation; and overviews of trends in the labor market for \u201cmiddle skills\u201d careers to inform academic planning.  \n\nFinancial Supports \u2013 up to $1,000 annually in participation-based stipends that incentivize engagement, as well as financial literacy education. enrichment grants. and last-dollar scholarships.  \n\nAcademic Supports \u2013 academic success and community building workshops and assistance transferring to a bachelor's degree program if desired \n\nIn the 2023-2024 academic year OMD served a total of 1921 students, an increase of 95% from the 987 served in the previous academic year, among these 1,120 were served through the expanded Campuswide Partnership with City Colleges of Chicago, 647 were served through the signature program at Harold Washington College, Wilbur Wright College, Harry S Truman College, Kennedy King College, and Richard J. Daley College. In the 2024-2025 academic year, OMD will serve over 2,400 students by continuing to serve new and continuing CW scholars at Olive-Harvey College, enrolling a second-year CW cohort at Malcom X College, and expanding the partnership to Harold Washington College. These students will likely be consistent with OMD\u2019s historic population demographics, with 46% of students served in the 2023-2024 academic year identifying as Latinx, 38% as African American, 6% as Asian/Pacific Islander, 5% as white, and 4% as Bi/Multi-racial or other. Furthermore, 66% of scholars were first-generation college students, 97% were identified as low-income by Pell grant eligibility, and 15% were recent high school graduates.",
