    "year_founded": "1993",
    "tax_id": "36-3900116",
    "contact_first_name": "John",
    "contact_last_name": "Pfeiffer",
    "contact_title": "Director of Philanthropy",
    "contact_email": "jpfeiffer@rssichicago.org",
    "leader_first_name": "Sandra",
    "leader_last_name": "Robinson",
    "leader_title": "Deputy Director of Philanthropy",
    "leader_email": "srobinson@rssichicago.org",
    "full_time_staff": "100",
    "part_time_staff": "5",
    "volunteers": "45",
    "mission": "Renaissance\u2019s vision is to create a Chicago without homelessness or inequities. Its mission is to empower Chicagoans for long-term success through innovative and comprehensive supportive services and housing.",
    "program_name": "",
    "program_description": "Renaissance Social Services, Inc. (Renaissance) is a leader in Chicago\u2019s response to homelessness. Renaissance prevents and ends homelessness for vulnerable Chicagoans, providing a range of proven solutions including prevention, outreach, rapid re-housing, permanent supportive housing, behavioral health care, and clinical case management. \n\nRenaissance provides care to unhoused individuals with or at risk for serious mental illnesses including individuals who have complex needs because of child welfare, carceral, or multi-system involvement; chronic medical conditions; substance-use disorders; and other factors.\n\nIn 2024, Renaissance\u2019s participants identified as 77% Black or African American, 9% White, 8% Hispanic, 0.5% Asian, 1% American Indian or Alaska Native, 0.5% Native Hawaiian or other Pacific Islander, and 4% multiple races. Renaissance\u2019s participants most commonly have a long history of being unhoused, have disabling conditions, low or no income, low or no assets, limited education and job training, and few traditionally marketable job skills. Participants come from community areas across the city of Chicago.\n\nRenaissance is a citywide leader in providing a wide range of evidence-based solutions to homelessness that are continuously improved with participant and team member input and data analysis and that have transformative impact.  Our programs include:\n\n\u2022\tHomelessness Prevention: Renaissance prevents homelessness in Chicago by providing short-term emergency funds to people at risk of losing their homes. The agency provides payment directly to the source for urgent financial hardships, such as car repairs or unexpected medical bills, which could result in missed rent payments and eviction. Additional referrals and support services are provided to link households to stabilizing resources in the community.\n\n\u2022\tFlexible Housing Pool Outreach & Case Management: As a key partner in Chicago's Flexible Housing Pool (FHP), Renaissance conducts outreach to homeless individuals and families targeted for housing by the FHP. The program locates and engages homeless people that have been identified as high utilizers of the health care system and engages them into support services, housing, and primary and preventative health care. The cost of this care is borne by local hospitals and health insurance providers that see the resulting reduction in health care expenses.\n\n\u2022\tStreet Outreach: Street outreach teams visit shelters and encampments on a regular basis to engage with people; provide food, clothing, and personal supplies; offer transportation to health care; and build relationships. Over time, growing trust allows team members to move people into housing and help them to engage in mental health and primary care services. \n\n\u2022\tRapid Re-Housing: For individuals and families who do not need intensive and long-term supports to exit homelessness and maintain permanent housing, Renaissance provides short-term housing assistance and case management that focuses on building individuals' income and strengths so that they can quickly return to housing independence and stability.\n\n\u2022\tPermanent Supportive Housing:  In partnership with landlords across Chicago, Renaissance operates permanent supportive housing programs, offering rent subsidies and wraparound supportive services to help participants increase their stability and autonomy. Renaissance maintains ongoing relationships with participants to ensure they can stay in their homes. Team members help them acquire mainstream benefits (including SNAP, TANF, and SSI), manage public transportation, find employment, learn how to maintain their homes (including paying rent, cooking, cleaning, and managing conflicts), create and manage budgets, and access support for their children's education.\no\tHousing Stability Program \u2013 site-based permanent supportive housing for single adults and families    \no\tFamily Support Program \u2013 scattered-site permanent supportive housing for families with children \no\tSupportive Housing Outreach Team \u2013 scattered-site permanent supportive housing for chronically homeless individuals with mental health diagnoses including military veterans \no\tSeniors Program \u2013 site-based and scattered-site permanent supportive housing for older adults\n\n\u2022\tBehavioral Health Services: To help participants move towards the greatest degree of stability possible, Renaissance provides critical mental health and substance use recovery services.\no\tCommunity Support Services provides clinical mental health services for people with mental health diagnoses in the community. Clinical case managers meet with program participants in their homes to help them understand and address behavioral health symptoms that are creating impediments or causing instability in their lives. \n\no\tHospital Engagement Pilot Program, located in the Austin community on Chicago\u2019s West Side, supports homeless adults following their discharge from nearby safety-net hospitals. Specially trained team members provide recovery support services, housing placement assistance, and connection to resources in a safe, inviting, and home-like atmosphere.",
