    "mission": "Thresholds provides innovative behavioral and primary healthcare that promotes empowerment, well-being, and full participation in community life. Through unwavering community-based engagement, support and advocacy, Thresholds helps people living with mental health and substance use conditions find home, health, and hope. \n\nFew, if any, agencies in the country have more experience than Thresholds in providing supportive services to people with serious mental illness (SMI) and co-occurring disorders. Founded in 1959, Thresholds is one of the largest providers of community mental health services in Illinois, serving over 9,500 people a year. The agency has 33 unique programs that employ 184 clinical teams, operating out of 80 sites (including 55 residential locations) throughout the Chicago metropolitan area. Our geographic reach spans five counties in Northeast Illinois.  \n\nOur clients are a diverse population that enter our system of care with no income and co-occurring conditions. Thresholds uses an intensive, team-based approach to address the full spectrum of our clients\u2019 complex health, housing, and social service needs. These teams deliver a wide range of services in our clients' natural settings (e.g., their apartment, at work, or community space). Whether the individual lives in Rogers Park or Blue Island, North Lawndale or the North Shore, our community support teams will provide the most comprehensive care model possible for them in their communities. Depending on needs and severity of symptoms, an individual can be connected to one of three levels of community support: the most intensive Assertive Community Treatment (ACT) team; the less intensive Community Support Team (CST); and the Community Support Individual (CSI), considered the final level of service before self-sufficiency. These models use a multidisciplinary team approach to provide intensive services where and when our clients need them\u201424 hours a day, seven days a week. All team models include peer support specialists, practitioners with expertise in psychiatry, nursing, social work, substance use treatment, and employment linkage who work closely together to provide integrated and outreach\u2010oriented services. These teams initiate the housing search, develop a Care Plan, and continue to work with clients over the long-term. Because team members share responsibility for providing treatment and rehabilitation services, the entire team supports each client's personalized goal of recovery.  \n\nThe Thresholds Centers for Mental Wellbeing provide a wide range of mental health and substance use treatment services to their immediate surrounding communities. Thresholds has four Centers for Mental Wellbeing, with locations in McHenry County, Kankakee County, Chicago\u2019s Ravenswood neighborhood, and the South Side of Chicago. The Centers for Mental Wellbeing follow the federal Substance Abuse and Mental Health Services Administration (SAMHSA) model of care delivery for Certified Community Behavioral Health Clinics (CCBHC). Thresholds is the only organization to operate four CCBHCs with federal grant funding in Illinois. \n\nThresholds is embarking on an ambitious three-year journey to elevate what it means to provide comprehensive care for individuals with mental health and substance use conditions while simultaneously expanding our service continuum and reach to impact more communities and deeply investing in our employees. The six pillars of the 2024-2026 strategic plan are as follows: \n\nStrategic Growth: Thresholds will generate sustainable growth by broadening the service continuum, serving new communities, ensuring access to services, creating affordable housing, building stronger communities, and cultivating transformative partnerships for greater impact. \n\nRacial Justice and Equity: We will promote equity and inclusion in internal practices, deepening commitment to equity and engagement in communities, and expanding services to Spanish-speaking communities. \n\nSystems Change: Thresholds will advocate for meaningful policy reforms and systemic change, while establishing a thriving Workforce Institute to address the behavioral health workforce shortage. \n\nService Delivery and Excellence: Thresholds will lead in elevating service excellence and clinical outcomes, cultivating a data-informed culture for better care, promoting equity and inclusion to ensure accessible integrated healthcare for all, and increasing health equity and access to technology for clients as integral components of our organizational mission. \n\nEmployee Investments: We will lead in championing employee wellbeing, strengthening employee engagement and retention, and developing leaders and mentors to fuel organizational growth as essential strategies for fostering a thriving workplace environment. \n\nSustainable Financial Future: Thresholds will prioritize funding streams to ensure stability and expanding fundraising efforts are key strategies for securing the financial future of the organization.",
    "program_name": "",
    "program_description": "Thresholds' project centers on the acquisition of the Glenn Apartments, a strategically located Single Room Occupancy (SRO) residence in Chicago\u2019s Uptown neighborhood, to preserve 100 units of naturally occurring, low-barrier affordable housing. The Glenn is a vital component of the housing ecosystem for individuals experiencing homelessness, situated near multiple transit lines and essential retail, medical, and supportive services. The current owner intends to list the property for sale, making Thresholds' acquisition crucial to prevent its conversion to market-rate or conventional, highly regulated affordable housing, which would displace the current vulnerable residents. The target population comprises very low-income individuals, including those with serious mental illness and substance use disorders, who often encounter significant barriers in accessing traditional subsidized housing due to paperwork, wait times, and limited supply. Thresholds currently master leases or occupies approximately 60 of the Glenn\u2019s 100 units, operating the Chicago Safe Haven and Front Door Diversion Programs within the building, serving individuals experiencing unsheltered chronic homelessness and those who would otherwise reside in nursing homes. Key activities include the acquisition of the Glenn Apartments, developing a comprehensive renovation scope to upgrade finishes, fixtures, lighting, security, and accessibility (addressing electrical service, roof, and windows), and maintaining the building's current operational model to avoid resident displacement. Thresholds will facilitate referrals from its clinical teams to vacant rooms, preserving the Glenn as a flexible, low-barrier housing resource. The project will also involve retaining existing management staff while adjusting for increased operating costs associated with professional management and facilities services. The anticipated upfront per-unit cost for acquisition and renovation is approximately $55,000 - $60,000, and the project is expected to be completed within 9-12 months, demonstrating a cost-effective and time-efficient approach compared to traditional affordable housing investments, such as the Lawson YMCA conversion which cost over $313,000 per unit and took more than 10 years.",
