    "mission": "Xchange Chicago, led by the Comer Science & Education Foundation and P33 Chicago, is a pioneering initiative aimed at advancing equity and inclusion in Chicago\u2019s tech economy. Our mission is to create a more inclusive economy through innovative workforce solutions. By addressing the unique challenges faced by historically underserved neighborhoods, Xchange aims to empower residents with the skills and opportunities needed to access high-earning jobs in thriving tech sectors. Our place-based model provides access to quality education and skills development, fosters entrepreneurship, supports social mobility and wealth building, and drives community investment to equitably benefit all Chicago residents.",
    "program_name": "",
    "program_description": "Xchange taps into the talent, potential, and commitment within Chicago\u2019s communities of color, leveraging the expertise of local organizations and community partners. Specifically, Xchange partners with minority-led IT suppliers who earn competitive contracts with companies looking to redirect some of their IT spend to train apprentices and create jobs that serve local communities.\n\nXchange\u2019s target population are adults, young adults and opportunity youth who live in mid-South Chicago, including Greater Grand Crossing, Woodlawn, Washington Park, South Shore, Avalon Park, Englewood, Chatham and Auburn Gresham communities. The priority geography we are serving is approximately 400,000 residents. Our focus is on individuals from low-income households who are unemployed, underemployed and/or career changers (seeking upward mobility through upskilling).\n \nThe population in Greater Grand Crossing (GGC), where Xchange is located, is 35,000 people, 97% of whom identify as Black/African American. Most residents lack a four-year degree, a substantial barrier to employment, particularly in tech. Only 21% of Grand Crossing residents have a bachelor\u2019s degree, 33% lower than the US average, and 50% lower than the Chicago metro area. The US Census and University of Chicago Medicine Community Health Needs Assessment of our service area have found that:\n1.\tGreater Grand Crossing is in the highest-ranking category for economic hardship and low child opportunity index;\n2.\t71% of households in our area have a median income of less than $37,000;\n3.\t40% of households receive food stamps;\n4.\t1 in 2 children live below the poverty line;\n5.\t55% of households possess a lack of economic stability; and \n6.\tnearly one-quarter of our residents over the age of 16 are unemployed.     \n\nXchange\u2019s focus on closing the opportunity gap is centered around three primary activities:\n\nKey Activity 1: Leveraging Apprenticeship Models\nWe are offering alternative tech skill training pathways that require only a high school degree/degree equivalent. Our training is an effective onramp to enter into high-wage tech roles, leading to apprenticeships with on-the-job training, and competitive salaries. Training consists of nondegree training courses and 2- and 4-year institutions offering continued education paths into apprenticeship roles. Once an individual completes training, they are hired by employer partners as paid apprentices working on contracted IT projects. After a 9 to 12-month apprenticeship, individuals convert into permanent hires.\n\nKey Activity 2: Building Wraparound Services for Supportive Onramps into Careers\nXchange offers wraparound support for individuals as they begin training and apprenticeships and start their path into tech careers, ensuring a holistic, sustained ecosystem of care. During training, individuals have access to supportive resources such as behavioral health and wellness services, financial literacy, transportation, and more. Xchange provides mentoring, career success coaching, and retention services to ensure professional development needs are met. Staff regularly check-in with participants and provide supportive services throughout training, apprenticeship, and employment.\n\nKey Activity 3: Developing Business Anchors in Grand Crossing\nXchange\u2019s inaugural site, a former warehouse building in Grand Crossing, has been renovated into a thriving commercial and retail hub where minority-led and mission-aligned businesses are contributing to neighborhood vitality. Two professional services tenants are leasing 80% of the building with office space for over 150 employees. The remainder of the building is dedicated for       new retail amenities such as catering operations and a caf\u00e9, and also space for community events, meetings and training. Xchange staff will be responsible for marketing the opportunities at Xchange and conducting outreach to ensure the potential economic benefits of the initiative are shared.",
